": "$P$", "MATH_0007": "$(T \\to B, \\mathcal{F})$", "MATH_0008": "$\\Cohstack_{X/B}$", "MATH_0009": "$\\Cohstack^P_{X/B}$", "MATH_0010": "$$\nP(n) =\n\\chi(X_t, \\mathcal{F}_t \\otimes_{\\mathcal{O}_{X_t}} \\mathcal{L}_t^{\\otimes n})\n$$", "MATH_0011": "$n \\in \\mathbf{Z}$", "MATH_0012": "$t \\in T$", "MATH_0013": "$I = \\mathbf{Z} \\to D(\\mathcal{O}_X)$", "MATH_0014": "$n \\mapsto \\mathcal{L}^{\\otimes n}$", "MATH_0015": "$n \\mapsto\n\\chi(X_t, \\mathcal{F}_t \\otimes_{\\mathcal{O}_{X_t}} \\mathcal{L}_t^{\\otimes n})$", "OWNARGEND_0001": "]\n\\label{example-hilbert-polynomial}\n", "REF_0001": "\\ref{situation-numerical}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{lemma-finite-list-perfect-objects}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{spaces-over-fields-lemma-numerical-polynomial-from-euler}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{example}[", "suffix": "\\end{example}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:401e22c00905e8fd95232f8535874be76fc27fb4ba4c4d66846755e558c2a8bb", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:e7055a8f2d78562e1f5bfe5b5ada488b279b46f870673f221bacb3d4017da2f8", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:8454dedaae9c86887d620aa415ab1187b4c0f3db9e78984e4385e71e681722d3", "source_text": "Coherent sheaves with fixed Hilbert polynomial<OWNARGEND_0001>Let <MATH_0001> be as in the introduction to this section.\nLet <MATH_0002> be an invertible <MATH_0003>-module.\nLet <MATH_0004> be a numerical polynomial.\nThen we can consider the open and closed algebraic substack\n<MATH_0005>\nconsisting of flat families of coherent sheaves with proper support\nwhose numerical invariants agree with <MATH_0006>: an object\n<MATH_0007> of <MATH_0008> lies in\n<MATH_0009> if and only if\n<MATH_0010>\nfor all <MATH_0011> and <MATH_0012>. Of course this is a\nspecial case of Situation <REF_0001>\nwhere <MATH_0013> is given by\n<MATH_0014>. It follows from\nLemma <REF_0002>\nthat this is an open and closed substack. Since the functions\n<MATH_0015>\nare always numerical polynomials (Spaces over Fields, Lemma\n<REF_0003>)\nwe conclude that\n<MATHSEG_0001> numerical polynomial<MATHSEG_0002>\nis a disjoint union decomposition.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:8a8e58439d6f610b1886664d8bc45837b96a276eaea5dd705f79df4896dc8778", "unit_id": "tag:0DNF:statement"}
